s=json.loads("\"\\\"Chocolate Numbers (https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.06093)\\\\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.1 years ago; This paper enumerates the number of ways to break an $m \\\\\\\\times n$ bar, which we call chocolate numbers, and introduces four new sequences related to these numbers.\\\\nTitle: Chocolate Numbers\\\\nAuthors: Caleb Ji, Tanya Khovanova, Robin Park, Angela Song\\\\nDate: Mon Sep 21 02:11:12 2015\\\\n\\\\nIn this paper, we consider a game played on a rectangular $m \\\\\\\\times n$ gridded chocolate bar. Each move, a player breaks the bar along a grid line. Each move after that consists of taking any piece of chocolate and breaking it again along existing grid lines, until just $mn$ individual squares remain. This paper enumerates the number of ways to break an $m \\\\\\\\times n$ bar, which we call chocolate numbers, and introduces four new sequences related to these numbers. Using various techniques, we prove interesting divisibility results regarding these sequences.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nPerturbation Effects on Word Lengths in Three-Reflection Symmetric Presentations of Dihedral Groups (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392980260_Perturbation_Effects_on_Word_Lengths_in_Three-Reflection_Symmetric_Presentations_of_Dihedral_Groups)\\\\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; For a generating set S composed of three involutions, none of which belong ... In this paper, we will prove this conjecture and, more signiﬁcantly, introduce an alternative ... how 3-reﬂection presentations can be used to write elements of the Dihedral group with\\\\n\\\\nshorter word length than is possible for presentations containing two generators.\\\\n\\\\nfor all symmetric generating sets consisting of three reﬂections as follows.\\\\n\\\\nConjecture 1. For a generating set S composed of three involutions, none of which belong\\\\n\\\\nto the order n cyclic subgroup of D n, we conjecture that λ 1(D n, S)≤ ⌊n\\\\n\\\\n2⌋+ 1.\\\\n\\\\nIn this paper, we will prove this conjecture and, more signiﬁcantly, introduce an alternative\\\\n\\\\nstrategy of studying λ 1(G, S) using techniques in additive combinatorics. We will also show\\\\n\\\\nhow 3-reﬂection presentations can be used to write elements of the Dihedral group with\\\\n\\\\nshorter word length than is possible for presentations containing two generators.\\\\n\\\\n2. A bound for prime n\\\\n\\\\nWe ﬁrst aim to establish a smaller bound than the one listed in Conjecture 1 in the special\\\\n\\\\ncase where n is prime, subject to a few restrictions on what S can be. Following the notation\\\\n\\\\nintroduced in Section 1, let W l be the set of elements of D n that can be written as words\\\\n\\\\nof length l by elements of S. Given that D n={e, r, r 2,·· ·r n−1, f, rf, r 2 f, · ··r n−1 f}, we\\\\n\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nThe parameter-dependence conjecture for peak-count positivity — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/331500/the-parameter-dependence-conjecture-for-peak-count-positivit)\\\\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The parameter-dependence conjecture for peak-count positivity ... source: William Kuszmaul, “Signed Enumeration of Upper-Right Corners in Path Shuffles”, arXiv:1510.00777 (2016).\\\\n\\\\n# \\\\n\\\\nThe parameter-dependence conjecture for peak-count positivity\\\\n\\\\nConjecture open\\\\n\\\\nLet $\\\\\\\\mathcal{V}$V be a positive vertical path with $u$u up-steps and $d$d down-steps, let $Q$Q be the set of quarter-planar paths with $r$r right-steps, $l$l left-steps, and vertical projection $\\\\\\\\mathcal{V}$V, and let $G_1$G 1​ be the peak-count generating function over $Q$Q. A polynomial is $(x+1)$(x+1)-positive when it belongs to $\\\\\\\\mathbb{N}_0[x+1]$N 0​[x+1]. The parameter-dependence conjecture. Whether $G_1$G 1​ is $(x+1)$(x+1)-positive depends only on $r$r, $l$l, $u$u, and $d$d. The claim is presented as a consequence of the equivalence conjecture together with the theorem that $G_2$G 2​ depends only on these four step counts; the paper notes that $G_1$G 1​ itself need not depend only on them.\\\\n\\\\n## Progress summary\\\\n\\\\nRefresh\\\\n\\\\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\\\\n\\\\nsource: William Kuszmaul, “Signed Enumeration of Upper-Right Corners in Path Shuffles”, arXiv:1510.00777 (2016).\\\\n\\\\nenumerative-combinatoricslattice-pathspositivity\\\\n\\\\n0\\\\n\\\\nposted by arXiv 2 days ago\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n## 0 Replies\\\\n\\\\n* * *\\\\n\\\\nSign in to reply.\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nRecall that (https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL19/Khovanova/khova9.pdf)\\\\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.4 years ago; ** *For the sequence \\\\\\\\(B_n\\\\\\\\) of chocolate-2 numbers the 2-adic value is bounded below, as in: \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\nu_2(B_n)\\\\\\\\ge n\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(n>1\\\\\\\\). ... Now let us consider a particular sequence \\\\\\\\((B_n)\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(B_n=A_{2,n}\\\\\\\\), or the number of ways to break a \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\times n\\\\\\\\) chocolate bar.\\\\nRecall that\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\\[\\\\nA_{m,n}=\\\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{in-1}A_{i,n}A_{m-i,n}+\\\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{im-1}A_{m,i}A_{m,n-i}.\\\\n\\\\\\\\]\\\\n\\\\nLooking at the first summand, note that for all \\\\\\\\(i\\\\\\\\neq 1\\\\\\\\) or \\\\\\\\(m-1\\\\\\\\), we have that\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\\[\\\\n\\\\\\\\nu_2\\\\\\\\!\\\\\\\\left(\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{in-1}A_{i,n}A_{m-i,n}\\\\\\\\right)\\\\\\\\ge \\\\\\\\nu_2(A_{i,n}A_{m-i,n})\\\\n\\\\\\\\]\\\\n\\\\\\\\[\\\\n\\\\\\\\ge (i+n-2)+(m-i+n-2)\\\\\\\\ge m+n-2\\\\n\\\\\\\\]\\\\n\\\\nas \\\\\\\\(n>2\\\\\\\\). For the other two terms \\\\\\\\(i=1, m-1\\\\\\\\), we have\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\\[\\\\n\\\\\\\\nu_2\\\\\\\\!\\\\\\\\left(\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{n-1}A_{1,n}A_{m-1,n}+\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{mn-n-1}A_{m-1,n}A_{1,n}\\\\\\\\right)\\\\n\\\\\\\\]\\\\n\\\\\\\\[\\\\n=\\\\\\\\nu_2\\\\\\\\!\\\\\\\\left(2\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{n-1}A_{1,n}A_{m-1,n}\\\\\\\\right)\\\\n\\\\\\\\]\\\\n\\\\\\\\[\\\\n\\\\\\\\ge \\\\\\\\nu_2(2A_{m-1,n})\\\\n\\\\\\\\]\\\\n\\\\\\\\[\\\\n\\\\\\\\ge 1+(m-1+n-2)=m+n-2,\\\\n\\\\\\\\]\\\\n\\\\nby the inductive hypothesis. Thus \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{in-1}A_{i,n}A_{m-i,n}\\\\\\\\) is a sum of terms with 2-adic order at least \\\\\\\\(m+n-2\\\\\\\\), so \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\nu_2\\\\\\\\!\\\\\\\\left(\\\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{in-1}A_{i,n}A_{m-i,n}\\\\\\\\right)\\\\\\\\ge m+n-2\\\\\\\\). Similarly, \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\nu_2\\\\\\\\!\\\\\\\\left(\\\\\\\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\\\\\\\binom{mn-2}{im-1}A_{m,i}A_{m,n-i}\\\\\\\\right)\\\\\\\\ge m+n-2\\\\\\\\), so \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\nu_2(A_{m,n})\\\\\\\\ge m+n-2\\\\\\\\), as desired, completing the inductive step.\\\\n\\\\nWe have already proven the result for \\\\\\\\(m=2\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(n=2\\\\\\\\). By the inductive step, if the statement is true for \\\\\\\\(n=2\\\\\\\\) and \\\\\\\\(m=i\\\\\\\\) for all \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\le i<k\\\\\\\\) for some \\\\\\\\(k>2\\\\\\\\), then the statement is true for \\\\\\\\(A_{k+1,3}\\\\\\\\) and by induction for all \\\\\\\\(A_{k+1,n}\\\\\\\\) for \\\\\\\\(n>2\\\\\\\\). Thus, the statement is true for all \\\\\\\\(m,n>2\\\\\\\\), as desired. □\\\\n\\\\nIn particular, it follows that all chocolate numbers except 1 are divisible by 2. We can also estimate 2-adic values for chocolate-2 numbers and chocolate square numbers.\\\\n\\\\n**Corollary 6.** *For the sequence \\\\\\\\(B_n\\\\\\\\) of chocolate-2 numbers the 2-adic value is bounded below, as in: \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\nu_2(B_n)\\\\\\\\ge n\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(n>1\\\\\\\\).*\\\\n\\\\n**Corollary 7.** *For the sequence \\\\\\\\(a(n)\\\\\\\\) of chocolate square numbers the 2-adic value is bounded below, as in: \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\nu_2(a(n))\\\\\\\\ge 2n-2\\\\\\\\).*\\\\n\\\\n## 5  Divisibility properties of chocolate-2 numbers\\\\n\\\\nNow let us consider a particular sequence \\\\\\\\((B_n)\\\\\\\\), where \\\\\\\\(B_n=A_{2,n}\\\\\\\\), or the number of ways to break a \\\\\\\\(2\\\\\\\\times n\\\\\\\\) chocolate bar. First, recall the following recursion:\\\\n\\\\n7--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nConstructive membership in black-box groups (https://maths.qmul.ac.uk/~raw/pubs_files/rybalg.pdf)\\\\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; at least two conjugacy classes of involutions, and a black-box encoding of length n. ... O(n3/2(ξ + ρ) log(1/ε) + nµ) we can construct the three involutions x, y, z, with probability of ... Since the dihedral group they generate lies in the\\\\ndifferent conjugacy classes. Thus we need at most O(n3/2 log(1/ε)) random elements before\\\\nwe ﬁnd one where xz has even order. Powering up to construct the involution takes time at\\\\nmost O(µn).\\\\n⊓⊔\\\\nWe now prove a similar result for groups having a unique class of involutions.\\\\nTheorem 10 Let G be a simple group of Lie type deﬁned over a ﬁeld of odd characteristic,\\\\nhaving a unique class of involutions, and a black-box encoding of length n. In time O((ξ +\\\\nρ) log(1/ε) + nµ) we can construct the three involutions x, y, z, with probability of success at\\\\nleast 1 −ε, for positive ε.\\\\nProof. We deduce from [19, Table 4.5.1] that the relevant groups are PSL2(q), PSL3(q), PSU3(q),\\\\nPSU4(q) for q ≡3 mod 8, PSL4(q) for q ≡5 mod 8, G2(q), 2G2(q), and 3D4(q).\\\\nThe Baer–Suzuki theorem [3, 39.6] implies that there exist two conjugates of an involution\\\\nwhose product has even order. We show that the proportion of pairs of involutions whose product\\\\nhas even order is at least a positive constant.\\\\nWe illustrate the method of proof with the example of G = 2G2(q). Observe that |G| = (q3 +\\\\n1)(q −1)q3 and the involution centraliser has order q(q2 −1). Hence the number of involutions\\\\nis a = q2(q2 −q + 1). We want to count the number of pairs of involutions whose product has\\\\neven order greater than 2 and dividing q + 1. Since the dihedral group they generate lies in the\\\\ncentraliser 2 × PSL2(q) of another involution, its normaliser is contained in H = 2 × Dq+1. The\\\\nnumber of conjugates of H is b = q3(q −1)(q2 −q + 1)/2. The number of such pairs in H is\\\\nc = (q + 1)(q −3)/4. Moreover, no pair is in two distinct conjugates of H. Hence the desired\\\\nproportion is\\\\nbc/a2 = (q −3)(q2 −1)/8q(q2 −q + 1) > (1 −3/q)/8 ≥1/9\\\\nsince q ≥27.\\\\n7--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nChocolate Numbers (https://www.academia.edu/143959276/Chocolate_Numbers?force_claim_to_highlight=true)\\\\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; By Euler’s totient theorem, we have rφ(p ) = r(p−1)p ≡ 1 (mod p)t , 15 t−1 giving Pn+(p−1)pt−1 ≡ r(p−1)p Pn ≡ Pn (mod p)t for all positive integers n, completing the statement for prime powers. ... In addition, we have further computational evidence supporting eventual periodicity modulo any number. Thus we have the following conjecture: Conjecture 22. ... Wright, An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers, Oxford University Press, 6th edition, 2008.\\\\n If the remainder when i=k ((4i − 5)2 − 5) is divided by p is not t t−1 0, then let it be r. By Euler’s totient theorem, we have rφ(p ) = r(p−1)p ≡ 1 (mod p)t , 15 t−1 giving Pn+(p−1)pt−1 ≡ r(p−1)p Pn ≡ Pn (mod p)t for all positive integers n, completing the statement for prime powers. Thus, splitting each positive integer m into a product of prime powers, the sequence Pn becomes divisible by some of them and is periodic modulo the rest of them, beginning from P1 . Thus, by the Chinese Remainder Theorem, multiplying all the periods together gives that Pn is periodic (mod m) for sufficiently large n, as desired. The eventual periodicity of Pn makes us expect the eventual periodicity of Bn . In addition, we have further computational evidence supporting eventual periodicity modulo any number. Thus we have the following conjecture: Conjecture 22. For any positive integer m, for sufficiently large indices, the sequence Bn becomes periodic modulo m. We also ran a lot of computational experiments that allowed us to make the following conjecture. This is supported by Lemma 20, assuming a connection between the behavior of Bn and Pn . Conjecture 23. If p is not 2, 5, or equivalent to ±1 (mod 5), then the sequence Bn modulo p repeats with a period divisible by p(p − 1). 6 Acknowledgments We would like to thank Prof. James Propp for the inspiration behind this paper and several useful discussions. Also, we are grateful to MIT PRIMES for providing the opportunity to conduct this research. Finally, we would like to thank Prof. Pavel Etingof for a helpful discussion. References [1] M. H. Albert, R. J. Nowakowski, and D. Wolfe, Lessons in Play: An Introduction to Combinatorial Game Theory, A. K. Peters/CRC Press, 2007. [2] W. N. Bailey, Generalized Hypergeometric Series, Cambridge, 1935. [3] E. R. Berlekamp, J. H. Conway, and R. K. Guy, Winning Ways for Your Mathematical Plays, Volume 1, Taylor and Francis, 2001. [4] G. H. Hardy and E. M. Wright, An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers, Oxford University Press, 6th edition, 2008. [5] E. L. Ince, Ordinary Differential Equations, Dover Publications, 1956. [6] J. G. Propp, Games of no strategy and low-grade combinatorics, http://faculty.uml.edu/jpropp/moves15.pdf. 16 [7] A.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nSigned Enumeration of Upper-Right Corners in Path Shuffles (https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.00777)\\\\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.1 years ago; Authors: William Kuszmaul ... Finally, we conjecture an equivalence between $(x+1)$-positivity of the generating function for upper-right-corner count and signed upper-right-corner count.\\\\nTitle: Signed Enumeration of Upper-Right Corners in Path Shuffles\\\\nAuthors: William Kuszmaul\\\\nDate: Sat Oct  3 05:41:18 2015\\\\n\\\\nWe resolve a conjecture of Albert and Bousquet-Melou enumerating quarter-plane walks with fixed horizontal and vertical projections according to their upper-right-corner count modulo 2. In doing this, we introduce a signed upper-right-corner count statistic. We find its distribution over planar walks with any choice of fixed horizontal and vertical projections. Additionally, we prove that the polynomial counting loops with a fixed horizontal and vertical projection according to the absolute value of their signed upper-right-corner count is $(x+1)$-positive. Finally, we conjecture an equivalence between $(x+1)$-positivity of the generating function for upper-right-corner count and signed upper-right-corner count.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\\\nA UNIFORM ACTION OF THE DIHEDRAL GROUP Z2 × D3 (https://www.mat.uc.pt/preprints/ps/p2439.pdf)\\\\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; The three involutions e, ρ, and ̺ are linear time equivalent to each other and ... strip of length ℓ(ν) −i + 1 in T e, with 1, . . . , νi −i + 1. ... tion 3.7, Q(rev bw) = Q( bw)E t = Q(w)E t = Q(σ0w)Et = Q((σ0w)♦•).\\\\n32\\\\nOLGA AZENHAS, ALESSANDRO CONFLITTI, AND RICARDO MAMEDE\\\\n= σ1.σ2σ1.σ3σ21235245125 = σ1.σ2σ1.σ31235345135 = σ1.σ2σ11245345145\\\\n= σ1.σ21245345245 = σ11245345345 = 1245345345 = (σ0w)♦.\\\\nIf one considers the alphabet [4], one has σ0w = σ1σ2σ1σ3σ2σ1w = 4422333444 but\\\\nstill (σ0w)♦= σ0w♦.\\\\nFrom this discussion, Section 3, and the computational complexity of the reversal\\\\ninvolution e [PV10], it then follows\\\\nTheorem 4.3. Let T be a LR tableau with shape λ/µ and word w. Let ̺ := ♦ρ = ♦•e =\\\\n•♦e = • e♦= ρ♦where ρ 